The mental model first (what most candidates miss): CORS is the BROWSER enforcing a SERVER's policy. Your fetch fired, the server responded — the browser REFUSED to hand the response to your JS because the server didn't declare your origin allowed. The console error is the browser's enforcement, not a network failure.

```js
// THE MECHANICS
// same origin = scheme + host + port all match
// http://app.com  vs  https://app.com  → different (scheme)
// http://app.com  vs  http://api.app.com → different (host)
// http://app.com:3000 vs http://app.com:8080 → different (port)

// SIMPLE requests (GET, POST+form types, no custom headers) — sent directly,
// browser checks the response header:
fetch('https://api.example.com/data');
// response must include: Access-Control-Allow-Origin: http://app.com (or *)
// missing/mismatched → browser blocks YOUR READING of it. Console: CORS error.

// PREFLIGHTED requests (PUT/DELETE, custom headers like Authorization,
// JSON Content-Type) — browser sends OPTIONS first:
// OPTIONS https://api.example.com/data
// server must answer:
//   Access-Control-Allow-Origin: http://app.com
//   Access-Control-Allow-Methods: PUT, DELETE
//   Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Content-Type, Authorization
//   (Access-Control-Max-Age: 86400 — cache the preflight, stop re-asking)
// OPTIONS rejected → the real request never even fires.
```

The fixes (in order of correctness):
1. Server sends the right headers — THE fix. Express: `cors({origin: 'http://app.com'})`; a list of allowed origins; or `*` for a genuinely public API.
2. Credentialed requests (cookies) need MORE: `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true` AND a SPECIFIC origin — `*` is forbidden with credentials (the browser rejects the combination; a common trap).
3. Same-origin the deployment: serve frontend and API from one origin (or subdomain with proper headers) — production apps often avoid CORS entirely by path (reverse proxy: /api → backend).
4. Dev-only: the Vite/webpack dev-server proxy — your code calls /api (same origin), the dev server forwards to the backend. This is NOT a production fix; it's a dev ergonomics tool that also prevents CORS noise in dev.

What does NOT work (the honesty test): JSONP (dead), 'disabling CORS in the browser' (a dev-machine flag, tests nothing real), and no-cors mode fetch (you can SEND cross-origin simple requests but never READ them — useless for APIs).
